{:title
 "Composition in the Smile Prompt Language, an Ordered Set of References Everything Is Made Of",
 :description
 "What a composition is in THE MAP of Smile, an ordered set of references to raw text and other compositions, nested without limit, with its settings, overrides and renders, and how my words reached that meaning, from a 2023 wish to break prompts into pieces to the two classes of August 2026, with every source.",
 :keywords
 "prompt composition, ordered references, nested compositions, prompt overrides, rendering as compiling, Smile Composer, modular prompts, history of Smile",
 :lede
 "In THE MAP, a composition is an ordered set of references, to raw text and to other compositions, nested without limit. Everything in the system is raw text or composition, and module, section, header, face and prompt are names a lens gives to positions.",
 :paras
 ["THE MAP gives a composition its settings, what it gathers and in what order, what is joined between the parts, which gathered parts are mirrored to close what they opened, the value of each variable, which lenses it may draw from, and its whitespace. An outer composition overrides an inner one, every override is stored as a new raw text so the library grows out of use, and rendering a composition is compiling it while decomposing is decompiling. A composition that sends is an emergence, the same kind with one difference."
 "The wish behind it is older than the word. In October 2023 I asked GPT-4 to break any prompt into its composable pieces and to separate structure from content, a precursor followed in [[t:precursor-the-archetypal-patterns-of-prompting]], before Smile had its faces or its system."
 "The first composer came in November 2025, with its own names for the parts. On 6 November, building a module from files, “You need to not rewrite any of the text I gave you.”[[m:2025-11-06]] The same day I reached for living tissue, “A composition of multiple cells is called tissue.”[[m:2025-11-06#3]] On 19 November the editor had to nest them, “I must be able to create compositions inside of compositions and all adding fucntionality must work.”[[m:2025-11-19]]"
 "In early 2026 the question was how pieces join well. “Combining together prompts modularity and composably doesn’t make sense, needs sections”.[[m:2026-02-13#2]]"
 "In July 2026 composition came back in the Smile project, on five days of that month. On 20 July, “I have laid out a system here for different tiers of prompt modules to be stored and composed.”[[m:2026-07-20]] On 25 July the name tag was composed of pieces placed by their ontology, and I named something the system has to carry, “this is a fundamental situation in Smile, that prompt instructions have a response format part.”[[m:2026-07-25]] On 26 July, “Compositions are where everything interrelates to each other, like a forest or something.”[[m:2026-07-26]] On 31 July I set what in all this is Smile, “the mechanism is not-Smile, but the resulting convention a model sees is Smile”.[[m:2026-07-31]] Through early August the settings arrived, nesting, ordering, pre-header and post-footer text, and gratitude as something composed, “gratitude is something you can automatically add to your compositions.”[[m:2026-08-05]]"
 "Between 11 and 13 August the system collapsed into the form THE MAP keeps. “Everything is either text or a composition of text.”[[m:2026-08-11]] “So every raw text saved is saved as a composition of 1”.[[m:2026-08-12]] “Isnt rendering the text from a composition compiling it and decomposing it decompiling it?”[[m:2026-08-13]] By the end of the month compositions were to render on every change, “why is the renders folder empty?”[[m:2026-08-29]]"
 "So composition moved from breaking prompts into pieces in 2023, through a composer of chains and documents in 2025, to one of the two things Smile is made of in August 2026. The other is [[t:raw-text]], derived compositions are [[t:linked-compositions]], and the names a lens gives to positions are followed in [[t:module]] and [[t:lens]]."],
 :faq
 [["What is a composition in Smile?"
 "An ordered set of references to raw text and other compositions, nested without limit, with settings for what it gathers, how parts are joined and mirrored, variables and overrides. Everything in Smile is raw text or a composition."]
 ["What is the difference between rendering and decomposing?"
 "Rendering a composition is compiling it into text, and decomposing is decompiling, carrying any text back into a set of compositions."]]}
